      "headline": "The EU AI Act is currently being phased in, with core obligations now in effect and high-risk system deadlines extended to 2027 and 2028.",
      "body": [
        "The EU AI Act officially entered into force on August 1, 2024, establishing a tiered risk-based regulatory framework. Since then, the legislation has been progressively implemented, with initial prohibitions on unacceptable-risk AI systems and literacy requirements coming online in early 2025.",
        "As of August 2, 2026, the regulation has entered a more intensive enforcement phase. This includes new transparency obligations for AI-generated content—such as watermarking for deepfakes and mandatory disclosures for chatbots—alongside the activation of full enforcement powers for the EU AI Office.",
        "Significant timeline shifts occurred in mid-2026 following the Digital Omnibus amendments, which deferred compliance deadlines for high-risk AI systems. Operators now have until December 2027 for stand-alone systems and until August 2028 for systems integrated into other products, allowing developers more time to align with safety standards.",
        "Non-compliance carries substantial financial risks, with potential fines reaching up to 35 million Euros or 7 percent of a company's total worldwide annual turnover, whichever is higher, reflecting the EU's commitment to strict enforcement."
      ],
